The problem asks us to count the number of pairs of mochi $(A_i, A_j)$ such that we can form a kagamimochi by placing $A_i$ on top of $A_j$. The condition for placing $A_i$ on top of $A_j$ is $A_i \le \frac{1}{2} A_j$. We are given $N$ mochi with sizes $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_N$. The mochi are sorted in ascending order, i.e., $A_1 \le A_2 \le \dots \le A_N$.

The input constraints are $N \le 5 \times 10^5$ and $A_i \le 10^9$. The time limit is likely around 2 seconds, so an $O(N^2)$ solution will be too slow. We need something closer to $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$.

Let's rephrase the condition. We want to find the number of pairs $(i, j)$ with $i \neq j$ such that $A_i \le \frac{1}{2} A_j$.
Wait, the problem says "You choose two mochi out of the N mochi, and place one on top of the other". It doesn't explicitly say $i < j$ or $i > j$. However, since the sizes are distinct entities (even if sizes are equal, they are different mochi), we are looking for pairs of indices $(i, j)$ with $i \neq j$.
The condition is $A_{\text{top}} \le \frac{1}{2} A_{\text{bottom}}$.
Since $A_i \ge 1$, if $A_{\text{top}} \le \frac{1}{2} A_{\text{bottom}}$, then $A_{\text{top}} < A_{\text{bottom}}$.
So, if we pick two mochi with sizes $x$ and $y$, we can form a kagamimochi if either $x \le y/2$ (placing $x$ on $y$) or $y \le x/2$ (placing $y$ on $x$).
However, the problem statement says "Given two mochi A and B, with sizes a and b respectively, you can make one kagamimochi ... by placing mochi A on top of mochi B if and only if a is at most half of b."
This implies the order matters for the condition, but the question asks "Find how many different kinds of kagamimochi can be made."
Usually, "different kinds" refers to the pair of mochi used. The note says "Two kagamimochi are distinguished if at least one of the mochi is different". This means we are counting pairs of indices $\{i, j\}$ where $i \neq j$ such that a valid stacking is possible.
